 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 1,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "ABERDEEN (Obar Dheathain). The Granite City. 'Obar Dheathain' means 'mouth of the Don'. The big grey buildings are built of local granite that sparkles in the sun, thanks to the mica. It sparkles for about an hour a year."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "MARISCHAL COLLEGE. The second largest granite building in the world, finished in 1906 with a spiky pinnacled front. It cost a fortune and looks it. It's now the city council offices, so you can walk in and pay your parking fine under a Gothic ceiling."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "ABERDEEN HARBOUR. Founded before 1136, it's Scotland's oldest business. The ferries to Orkney and Shetland sail from here, and so do the supply boats for the oil rigs. Look out for the dolphins at Torry Battery, and the pungent smell of the fish market."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "THE MARITIME MUSEUM. A whole offshore oil rig in a hall, a model of a clipper ship called the Thermopylae and a very sensible hat."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "UNION STREET. A mile of granite built on a bridge over a ravine, so the shops on the street don't know that underneath them there's a valley. Hope the foundations don't, either."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "FOOTDEE (Fittie). A tiny seaside village of fishermen's cottages arranged in squares, with their backs to the North Sea gales. They've been rebuilt many times, and are still lovely."
  }
 ],
